# Continued fractions and the Farey structure of the rationals, the arithmetic of best approximation. Writing a number as
# an integer plus one over an integer plus one over another peels off its nested self-similarity, and truncating gives the
# convergents, the fractions that approximate it better than any with a smaller denominator. The golden ratio has the
# simplest continued fraction of all, every layer a one, so it is approached the most slowly and is in that sense the most
# irrational number, and its convergents are exactly the ratios of consecutive Fibonacci numbers. Two convergents in a row,
# and more generally two Farey neighbors, satisfy the unimodular relation that the cross difference of their numerators and
# denominators is one, which is why their Ford circles are tangent and why the mediant, formed by adding numerators and
# adding denominators, lands cleanly between them in the Stern-Brocot tree. This file checks the unimodular relation for a
# Farey pair, the mediant construction, and the Fibonacci-convergent unimodular identity that pins the golden ratio.

# Farey neighbors one half and two thirds are unimodular: the cross difference 2*2 - 1*3 = 4 - 3 = 1, so their Ford
# circles are tangent.
hold farey-neighbors-are-unimodular
  call is-equal
    code 1
    call subtract
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 2
      call multiply
        code 1
        code 3

# The mediant of one half and one over one is two thirds: numerators add, 1 + 1 = 2, and denominators add, 2 + 1 = 3, the
# Stern-Brocot child between the two parents.
hold mediant-numerator-adds
  call is-equal
    code 2
    call add
      code 1
      code 1

hold mediant-denominator-adds
  call is-equal
    code 3
    call add
      code 2
      code 1

# Consecutive Fibonacci convergents of the golden ratio are unimodular: five thirds and eight fifths satisfy
# 5*5 - 8*3 = 25 - 24 = 1, the Cassini identity, so each is the best rational approximation at its size.
hold golden-convergents-are-unimodular
  call is-equal
    code 1
    call subtract
      call multiply
        code 5
        code 5
      call multiply
        code 8
        code 3
